Subject: [PATCH] spmi: msm: skip unused APID map entries

Zero APID map entries describe unused channels. Treating them as SID 0,
peripheral 0 marks an invalid channel as usable and may overwrite a real
PPID 0 mapping with the last unallocated APID. Skip them before mapping
or reading their ownership registers.

Primary reference: Linux v6.12 drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c#L1189-L1200
pmic_arb_read_apid_map_v5() explicitly skips a zero map register before
extracting the PPID. The older-generation pmic_arb_find_apid() does so too:
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/drivers/spmi/spmi-pmic-arb.c#L1105-L1115

diff --git a/drivers/spmi/spmi-msm.c b/drivers/spmi/spmi-msm.c
--- a/drivers/spmi/spmi-msm.c
+++ b/drivers/spmi/spmi-msm.c
@@ -347,6 +347,10 @@
 		uint8_t slave_id = (periph & 0xf0000) >> 16;
 		uint8_t pid = (periph & 0xff00) >> 8;
 
+		/* A zero APID map entry is unused, not SID 0 / peripheral 0. */
+		if (!periph)
+			continue;
+
 		switch (priv->arb_ver) {
 		case V2:
 		case V3:
